  title: [World War I: Causes, Conflict and Consequences],
  subject: [History],
  grade: [Grade 10],
  duration: [1 hour 30 minutes],
  instructions: [Answer *all* questions. Refer to the sources where asked. Use your own knowledge to support your answers.],
)

= Section A: Multiple choice

#question(points: 1)[
  Which event is generally considered the immediate trigger of the First World War?
  #mcq([The sinking of the _Lusitania_], [The Battle of Verdun], [The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand], [The Treaty of Versailles], answer: 3)
]

#question(points: 1)[
  Which alliance did Italy join in 1882 before switching sides in 1915?
  #mcq([The Triple Entente], [The Triple Alliance], [The Schlieffen Alliance], [The Dual Monarchy], answer: 2)
]

#question(points: 1)[
  The German plan to defeat France quickly by passing through Belgium was called the:
  #mcq([Zimmermann Plan], [Schlieffen Plan], [Marshall Plan], [Dawes Plan], answer: 2)
]

= Section B: Source-based questions

Read Source A and Source B before answering.

#callout(kind: "example", title: [Source A])[
  #quote(block: true)[
    The Entente and the Central Powers each believed that a short war was possible. Mobilisation
    timetables, once begun, could not easily be halted, and every government feared that delay
    would leave it at a disadvantage.
  ]
  Adapted from a modern military history textbook.
]

#callout(kind: "example", title: [Source B])[
  #quote(block: true)[
    We were told that this was a war to end all wars. Our generals promised victory by Christmas.
    Instead we lived in mud for four years, and I have seen men drown in shell-holes within sight of
    their own trenches.
  ]
  Extract from the memoir of a British infantry officer, published 1930.
]

#question(points: 2)[
  + Identify one reason given in Source A for why the powers expected a short war.
    #answer-lines(count: 1, answer: [Mobilisation timetables could not easily be halted, and governments feared delay would disadvantage them.])
  + Explain what Source B suggests about the gap between wartime promises and experience.
    #answer-lines(count: 2, answer: [Source B suggests the promise of a quick victory was false: the war lasted four years of trench fighting and mud, so the officer felt the promises had been broken.])
]

#question(points: 4)[
  Compare the views of Source A and Source B on why the war lasted so long. Use details from both sources.
  #answer-lines(count: 6, answer: [Source A focuses on the planning and mobilisation systems that made the war hard to stop, while Source B focuses on the lived experience of trench warfare and the failed promises of generals. Source A explains the origins of the war's momentum; Source B shows its human cost, so they address different aspects rather than the same question.])
]

#question(points: 4)[
  Using Source B and your own knowledge, assess how useful this source is to a historian studying the experience of soldiers on the Western Front.
  #answer-lines(count: 6, answer: [Strengths: a first-hand account by an officer with direct experience, showing emotional impact. Limitations: written and published in 1930, after the war, so memory and purpose may shape it; one officer's perspective may not represent all soldiers. Good answers weigh these points and reach a judgement.])
]

= Section C: Essay

#question(points: 15)[
  "The First World War was caused more by the alliance system than by any single event."
  To what extent do you agree with this view? Support your answer with evidence.
  #answer-space(height: 20em)
]

#rubric(
  ([Argument], [Clear, sustained judgement that answers the question.], [Clear judgement, partly sustained.], [Descriptive, little judgement.], [No argument.]),
  ([Evidence], [Accurate, specific and well chosen.], [Mostly accurate and relevant.], [General or partly inaccurate.], [Absent or wrong.]),
  ([Analysis], [Explains causation and weighs factors.], [Some explanation of causes.], [Lists facts with little explanation.], [Missing.]),
  points: (5, 5, 5, 0),
)